---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
import '../styles/prose.css';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Alp builds AI systems in Munich. Three years of enterprise software, three languages, and a habit of learning by building."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="label">About</p>
      <h1>I'm Alp. I learn by building things.</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I live in Munich and build AI systems: agents that run on their own, retrieval that
        answers questions from documents, and the tests that tell me whether either one is any
        good. Most of what I know, I learned by building something and then finding out where
        it broke.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before AI, I spent three years at Flexus in Würzburg building enterprise software with
        SAP CAP, on a fleet management product that won an international SAP Innovation Award.
        I owned the backend architecture and the main screen people worked in, and later its
        roadmap too. It was a good job. It taught me something most AI demos skip: what it
        takes for software to hold up in front of people who did not choose it and cannot work
        around it.
      </p>

      <h2>Why I started over</h2>
      <p>
        I could see which half of my skills would matter most in ten years, and it was not the
        half I was being paid for.
      </p>
      <p>
        So in June 2026 I set myself a sixteen-week program: LLM applications, retrieval,
        agents, evaluation, fine-tuning. No course and no certificate at the end, just one rule:
        every block had to end in a repository that runs. That rule is why
        <a href="/work/sage">Sage</a> has a test set I labelled by hand instead of a demo video,
        and why my <a href="/work/munich-apartment-agent">apartment agent</a> can resume after a
        crash instead of only working in a screenshot.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Every project keeps its own notes.</strong> Each repository carries a short
        record of the decisions I made, the alternatives I turned down, and the problems that
        cost me a day. I write it so that someone, or some model, starting cold can pick up
        from the conclusions instead of rereading the whole history.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Ideas get argued with before they get built.</strong> I think in Obsidian. A
        half-formed idea gets taken apart there before it costs anything. What survives moves
        into a project's notes; most ideas stay notes, and that is fine.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>I start with the parts I can measure.</strong> Whether an answer "sounds good"
        is a matter of taste, and taste does not settle arguments. Whether retrieval found the
        right passage is a number on a test set, and it either went up or it did not. That is
        why almost everything I build has an evaluation in it, often one that took longer than
        the thing it measures.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>A system whose output you cannot check is not finished. It is a demo with good lighting.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Three languages, three countries</h2>
      <p>
        Turkish is my first language. I learned German at school in Turkey, arrived in Germany
        at B2, reached C2 within a year, and then did a whole Bachelor's degree in it. English I
        learned properly in the preparation year at ODTÜ in Ankara, where I got in after placing
        in the top 0.15% of the Turkish university entrance exam.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of it was a straight line, and I have stopped pretending it should have been.
        Changing university, country and field each cost time, and each is the reason I can do
        something today that I could not before. The <a href="/journey">journey page</a> has
        the full sequence.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p>
        This October I start an MSc in Information Systems (Wirtschaftsinformatik) at the
        Technical University of Munich, to learn the mathematics under the models and not just
        the APIs on top of them. Alongside it I keep building
        <a href="/work/advocate">Advocate</a> and writing down what I learn.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are hiring for AI engineering, or you have a hard, loosely defined problem that
        needs building, <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>write to me</a>. You can also
        <a href="/cv/ali-alp-oezer.pdf" download="Ali-Alp-Oezer-CV.pdf">download my CV</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-head { padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-lg); }
  .page-head .label { margin-bottom: var(--space-sm); }
  h1 {
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  .prose { padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl); }
</style>
